media: h713-hdmirx: a raster in no record is unlisted whatever the record says

0136m gave "the firmware's mode table has no record for this raster" its own
answer, -ERANGE, and the HY310 run of 22.09.2026 showed that 1600x900 still
does not reach it (umbau/test-20260915/dev20-20260922.md, row "Q3 0136m
1600x900"): the status stayed on "change in flight", the ring stayed at the
previous mode's 1366x768, and the journal had no line at all.

Why it does not reach it. The mode before 1600x900 was 1366x768. The display
firmware locks a raster it knows, and it leaves its record at the last one it
locked - so with 1600x900 on the wire the record still reads 1366x768 while
INCAP +0x874 reads 1600x900. That is exactly the shape of the cross-check
0136f put in ahead of everything else:

	if (have_sig && !own_record &&
	    (sig.h_size != width || sig.v_size != height))
		return -ENOLCK;

and it is right for a mode change in flight, where the record catches up a
poll or two later. It is wrong here, because the record will never catch up:
there is no record to catch up to. The test fires first, on every poll, and
the unlisted branch at the end of the detection is never reached.

What this patch does. Where that cross-check would fire, the raster INCAP
names is looked up in the firmware's table first, with the frame rate
measured on the flip pointers - the stale record's rate belongs to the mode
before it, so it is not used. If the table is loaded and no record carries
that raster, the answer is h713_hdmirx_unlisted() right there, whatever the
stale record says. If a record does carry it, the -ENOLCK stays: that is a
change in flight towards a mode this receiver can lock, and one or two more
polls settle it.

The lookup is h713_hdmirx_tse_match() on a scratch timing, so it is the same
rule the locking path applies further down - active size for the records that
carry one, totals for the rest, and the frame rate unless the record switches
that check off. Both scan types are tried, because INCAP carries none and the
stale record's belongs to the other mode; a raster any record carries under
either scan type stays on the -ENOLCK path. Without the table (no
allwinner,firmware-tse) nothing changes at all, and nothing changes while the
flip pointers give fewer than two frames to measure a rate from.

The three cases of the dev19 and dev20 runs, on paper:

  1600x900 after 1366x768. Record 1366x768 stale, INCAP 1600x900, flips
  ~60 Hz. No record in the 152-record table carries 1600x900 under either
  scan type, so: -ERANGE, one line "source 1600x900 at 60.00 Hz: in none of
  the firmware's mode records -- no lock". The line is printed once because
  h713_hdmirx_unlisted() keys on the INCAP active word and the firmware will
  never lock this raster, so that word does not move. h713-tv hides the plane
  and says "not in the firmware's table". Today: silent -ENOLCK for ever.

  1024x768 after 1366x768. Same shape, but 1024x768 IS in the table (it
  locked on the device, dev20 mode list). The lookup finds it, -ENOLCK as
  before, and the poll after the record catches up locks the mode. Unchanged.

  720p60 after 1080p60. Same: 1280x720 at 60 is in the table, -ENOLCK for the
  polls the record lags by, then a lock. Unchanged.

0136i's lock and forget rules are untouched: the new branch sits where the
old -ENOLCK sat, before h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry() and before the lock
memory is compared, and it neither sets nor drops a lock.

Source: umbau/test-20260915/dev20-20260922.md (row "Q3 0136m 1600x900" and
the verdict line for Q3), umbau/work/Q3/REPORT.txt section 1.2.
Follow-up to 0136m, which stays byte-identical; 0136h, 0136i and 0136k too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -3519,6 +3519,45 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_unlisted(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 active,
 	return -ERANGE;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Does the firmware's table carry the raster INCAP is writing, at all?
+ *
+ * 0136m asks that at the end of the detection, after a timing has been built
+ * and a record has had its chance to match it. The 1600x900 run never got
+ * that far (dev20, row "Q3 0136m 1600x900"): the mode before it was 1366x768,
+ * the firmware left its record at that geometry, and the stale-record test
+ * answered "change in flight" on every poll while INCAP named 1600x900 and
+ * nothing was in flight at all. The record is about the mode before it and
+ * cannot decide that case; INCAP is what the capture is writing now and can.
+ *
+ * The lookup is h713_hdmirx_tse_match() on a scratch timing, so it is the
+ * same rule the locking path uses below - active size or totals per record,
+ * and the frame rate unless the record skips it. Both scan types are tried:
+ * INCAP carries none and the stale record's belongs to the other mode, and a
+ * raster any record carries has to stay on the -ENOLCK path.
+ */
+static bool h713_hdmirx_tse_carries(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, unsigned int width,
+				    unsigned int height, unsigned int htotal,
+				    unsigned int vtotal, unsigned int fps_x100)
+{
+	struct v4l2_dv_timings probe;
+	unsigned int scan;
+
+	for (scan = 0; scan < 2; scan++) {
+		memset(&probe, 0, sizeof(probe));
+		probe.type = V4L2_DV_BT_656_1120;
+		probe.bt.width = width;
+		probe.bt.height = height;
+		probe.bt.interlaced = scan ? V4L2_DV_INTERLACED
+					   : V4L2_DV_PROGRESSIVE;
+		if (h713_hdmirx_tse_match(rx->tse_modes, rx->tse_count, &probe,
+					  htotal, vtotal, fps_x100 * 10))
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * What the source is sending, from the three things the ARM can see:
  *
@@ -3538,8 +3577,8 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_unlisted(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 active,
  *    writing right now (doku/nachtlog/E4). The record has no totals, so the
  *    pixel clock and the blanking come from here -- and the block is the
  *    cross-check: a record that names another active size than the block is
- *    a change in flight, and the answer is "not locked yet" (-ENOLCK), not
- *    one of the two.
+ *    a change in flight, and the answer is "not locked yet" (-ENOLCK) -- or
+ *    "unlisted" where the block's raster is in no record at all (0136r).
  *
  * The blanking is known as a whole, not split: neither the record nor the
  * block carries the sync positions. A preset that matches settles that; a
@@ -3642,8 +3681,19 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 		return -ENOLCK;
 	}
 	if (have_sig && !own_record &&
-	    (sig.h_size != width || sig.v_size != height))
+	    (sig.h_size != width || sig.v_size != height)) {
+		unsigned int seen = 0;
+
+		/* the stale record's rate is the mode before it; measure */
+		if (flips.flips >= 2)
+			seen = div64_u64(100ULL * NSEC_PER_SEC, flips.period_ns);
+		if (rx->tse_count && seen &&
+		    !h713_hdmirx_tse_carries(rx, width, height, htotal, vtotal,
+					     seen))
+			return h713_hdmirx_unlisted(rx, active, seen,
+					"in none of the firmware's mode records");
 		return -ENOLCK;
+	}
 
 	h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry(rx, width, height, rowbyte, ring);
 
